gion $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=3,\\,{x}^{2}y=6,\\,xy=2,\\,xy=6\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=6\\,⟹\\,u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & xy=6\\,⟹\\,v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a42d50fe797c9450b27acde75ac7b69ed3e4ea5acc6c9dc93cd6df18b9ed1882.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)\\cdot (6-2) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fb765d-ae64-5832-b007-b74f3340d57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557448} {:answer/id #uuid "62aef600-0c78-54d8-81c7-8d4524fdf63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557464} {:answer/id #uuid "9945870d-1803-5fe4-9bf3-865f89d3cd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557475} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b850bc-8ee1-5ce0-802a-5fddf2d584a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557496} {:answer/id #uuid "fe95aa6e-580a-552f-be09-ab51db51fae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8b850bc-8ee1-5ce0-802a-5fddf2d584a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557496} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1862914-74d2-5d9e-9a9b-863b6c76ac81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd0b89a-d3f8-5c0c-990c-72bcaf568dcc" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{4}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=4,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 5,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/56/560d4d074d9d07eb034d92b8f1dfabfc3e59d659c91d80c3fedf68367a575e41.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5-4)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f03a8f3-09d3-5671-890f-62e7bfebe2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557457} {:answer/id #uuid "6a93d690-2db3-54db-8e20-261a49e04fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557467} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c76cd2-18bc-54c4-bec6-41c9e208d51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557480} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff2435-f7c2-57f1-a4e1-b9ad0070da42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557490} {:answer/id #uuid "f72a6040-f6eb-5203-b778-5f0e5d0eb63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f72a6040-f6eb-5203-b778-5f0e5d0eb63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557495} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f802c8-f27c-572f-a462-5834e5ade5cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df897641-0828-516d-b42d-f7e72c630377" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\,xy=7,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & xy=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/cf/cf598751d1bb1edda1bb65fd5b94d2ad5805289c48716f69a71394fd3684e920.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (7-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9a309f-94bc-51a2-869f-3b3a9041caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9a309f-94bc-51a2-869f-3b3a9041caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557695} :answer-field/id #uuid "2358b114-00ed-5898-b286-205889192af5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557636} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe8cfd3-e0cc-5393-a292-993c1116bc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{41}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe8cfd3-e0cc-5393-a292-993c1116bc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{41}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557641} :answer-field/id #uuid "677c5a85-d33c-5340-9e9f-244d2e3567b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 